{{Politics of Balisca}}
'''Bayaran alienation''' ({{langx|an|Alienación bayarana}}; {{langx|bl|Alienación bayarana}}) refers to the sentiment of political, economic, and cultural marginalization experienced by the community and former republic of [[Bayara]] within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. Expressions of Bayaran alienation allege that the [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]], dominated by the [[Levante, Balisca|Levantine]] political and economic establishment centered on [[Castejón]], has systematically favored the eastern coast at the expense of the western interior, extracting Bayara's wealth and resources while denying it commensurate political influence or investment. The phenomenon has its roots in the repeated devastation of Bayaran territory during the [[Reapers' War]] (1578–1608), the [[Maroon Wars]], the [[Pastry War]], and the [[Baliscan Civil War]] (1828–1836), as well as the imposition of emancipation (without [[W:Compensated emancipation|compensation]]), land redistribution, and [[Reconstruction reforms|Reconstruction policies]] over Bayaran objections during the [[Anyos Federales|Federal Years]].

Unlike the more diffuse regional grievances found in other parts of the country, Bayaran alienation is distinguished by its depth and its historical specificity: it draws on lived memory of communities destroyed, populations displaced, and sovereignty curtailed by a confederal government that many Bayarans perceive as having first abolished their labor system, then removed their racial hierarchy, and finally subjected them to military occupation under the guise of reconstruction. The sentiment has manifested across the political spectrum, from demands for greater provincial autonomy and a fairer share of confederal revenues to the secession crisis of 1828 and, in its most extreme forms, calls for the complete dissolution of the confederal bond. 

Bayaran alienation remains a severely contested concept in Baliscan historiography, owing to the degree to which it is intertwined with the defense of [[W:Chattel slavery|chattel slavery]] and the [[Maroon resettlement|Maroon removal campaign]], which modern scholarship universally characterizes as [[W:Ethnic cleansing|ethnic cleansing]] and [[W:Settler genocide|settler genocide]].

== Origins ==

The roots of Bayaran alienation predate the Confederation itself. From the earliest colonial period, in which the [[Marañón basin]] served as the battleground for conflicts whose causes originated elsewhere and whose resolutions were dictated by distant powers with little regard for Bayaran interests. This pattern of external imposition — of violence fought on Bayaran soil, of settlements imposed from without, and of social orders dismantled by decree — established the foundational experience from which Bayaran alienation would develop.

=== The Reapers' War ===

{{Main|Reapers' War}}

The [[Reapers' War]] (1578–1608), which pitted Jacano-speaking Catholic colonists against Volisanian-speaking [[Catharism|Catharist]] communities and their Indigenous allies, was fought primarily on Bayaran soil. The conflict resulted in the massacre and forced expulsion of Catharist populations — the Rodamóns — from the [[Marañón basin|Marañón basin]], the ''de facto'' partition of Bayara along ethnoreligious lines under the [[Treaty of Navassa]], and the destruction of settlements and infrastructure that would take generations to rebuild. For Bayarans, the war established the pattern that would recur throughout their history: Bayara served as the battleground for conflicts whose causes originated elsewhere, and whose resolutions were dictated by distant powers with little regard for Bayaran interests.

=== Maroon Wars and abolition ===

{{Main|Maroon Wars|Granyana Proclamation}}
[[File:Capitao-mato.jpg|left|thumb|334x334px|''Cuarenta acres y una mula'' {{Circa|1823}}, depicting newly emancipated Kafto-Bayaran landowners in [[Macaio Province]]]]
The [[Maroon Wars]], which spanned from the earliest slave revolts following the introduction of slavery in Bayara in 1526 to the destruction of the institution in 1809, compounded this pattern of devastation. The wars were fought overwhelmingly on Bayaran territory, where the plantation economy had concentrated the largest enslaved population in the Northern Triangle. The success of the Maroon resistance — enabled by the Catharist sanctuary in [[Cerdanya]], the literacy of enslaved Muslim Kaftians, and the military capabilities of Owuro and Imfezzan war captives — resulted in the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] and the 1809 [[Granyana Proclamation|Granyana Convention]], which ''de jure'' abolished slavery across the [[Northern Triangle]]. The destruction of the former slave-owning class, and the consolidation of Maroon control in [[Araucaria]] represented a fundamental restructuring of Bayaran society that the planter elite experienced as a catastrophe.

For Bayara's white and ''mestizo'' landholding class, abolition was not merely an economic blow — it was the destruction of a social order they had built over three centuries. The Granyana Convention's abolition decree, though issued by the [[Northern Confederation]], was perceived in Bayara as the work of Catharist missionaries, Maroon insurgents, and confederal authorities who had no understanding of — or concern for — the consequences for Bayara's economy and social hierarchy. The resentment generated by abolition would prove to be one of the most durable sources of Bayaran alienation, feeding directly into the secession crisis and the Maroon removal campaign.

=== The Pastry War and the Cerdanyan Legion ===

{{Main|Pastry War|Cerdanyan Legion}}

The [[Pastry War]], triggered by the murder of a Volisanian pastry chef in [[Xàbia]] by a Catholic mob and the cycle of violent reprisals that followed, reinforced the pattern of Bayara as battleground for external conflicts. The intervention of the [[Cerdanyan Legion]] — a confederal peacekeeping force — and the subsequent partition of Bayara under the [[Treaty of Navassa]] were experienced by Bayarans as the imposition of foreign authority over their territory. The Legion's presence in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]] was viewed by most rural Bayarans as an illegitimate military occupation, a sentiment that would prove decisive in driving the secession crisis.

When Bayaran delegates blocked confederal funding for the Legion in the [[Confederal Congress]] in 1826, forcing its withdrawal, the act was both a fiscal maneuver and a declaration that Bayara would no longer subsidize its own occupation. The withdrawal of the Legion, however, left the Estuarian lowlands without a peacekeeping presence, and the inter-communal violence that the Legion had suppressed resumed almost immediately — providing the confederal government with further justification for intervention and reinforcing the cycle of occupation and resistance that characterized Bayara's relationship with the Confederation.

== Bayaran secession crisis and the Maroon removal ==

{{Main|Baliscan Civil War|Maroon resettlement|Carpinchera Republic}}

The [[Compromise of 1827]], which had sought to reform the confederal structure and address the [[Confederal Question]], was rejected by the majority of Bayaran delegations — not because they opposed reform, but because they viewed the proposed strengthening of the central government as an unacceptable infringement on the sovereignty they had never willingly surrendered. The subsequent secession of Bayara under the [[Xunta Nacionalista]] and its renunciation of the [[Articles of Confederation]] on 6 July 1828 was the direct political expression of accumulated alienation.
[[File:Flag of Carpinchera.svg|thumb|The flag of the [[Carpinchera Republic]], now adopted as the flag of the [[Carpinchera|region of Carpinchera]]]]
The secession, however, was not unanimous. The city of [[Macaio]], which economically dominated the Republic and generally favored [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]], refused to recognize the Xunta. Urban Macaio's commercial interests, tied to the entrepôt trade and the [[Hesperian League]]'s commercial networks, were fundamentally at odds with the agrarian nationalism of the Xunta. The [[Carpinchera Republic]], proclaimed on 6 July 1828 under President [[Martín Ferrández]], was a pro-confederation, anti-separatist breakaway state that militarily controlled Macaio and its immediate hinterland for fourteen months. Though never explicitly recognized by the Confederation, the Carpinchera Republic played a key role in disrupting the secessionist war effort and demonstrated that Bayaran alienation was never a universal sentiment — it was contested within Bayara itself, divided along lines of class, geography, and economic interest.
[[File:Guerillas rugendas.jpg|left|thumb|''Bandeirantes'' by [[Johann Moritz Rugendas]]]]
The most consequential act of the Xunta Nacionalista was the adoption of the [[Maroon resettlement|Maroon removal]] as official state policy. Building on decades of settler pressure and the expansionist desire to acquire Indigenous land in the Abyalan interior, the Xunta formalized the forced displacement of approximately 150,000 freedmen and free persons of color, along with 60,000 members of the Honú-Abá, Murí, Aruanãyará, Roáka'á, and Kalútupí nations, expelling them to the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. The removal, which modern scholarship characterizes as ethnic cleansing and settler genocide, was driven by anti-Black and anti-Indigenous racism, Christian supremacy, and the desire to erase the social restructuring imposed by abolition. It intensified the confederal crisis, ultimately triggering the [[Basilio mutiny]] and the outbreak of the [[Baliscan Civil War]].

=== Baliscan Civil War ===
{{Main|Baliscan Civil War|Anyos Federales|Treaty of Bixessarri}}

The [[Baliscan Civil War]] (1828–1836), fought overwhelmingly on Bayaran territory, represented the most devastating chapter in the pattern of Bayaran devastation. Bayaran secessionist forces were forced to divert significant numbers of troops toward subduing the intensifying uprisings and ambushes by Maroons and [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]] resisting the Xunta's policy of forced removal from the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. The internal displacement of confederal citizens from one constituent republic to another constituted a confederal crisis, providing the confederal government with a ''casus belli'' that went beyond Bayaran secession: the Xunta was not merely withdrawing from the confederation, but actively violating the rights of citizens of another republic. 
[[File:Banks’s Expedition; burning of the state capitol of Louisiana, Baton Rouge, Tuesday night, December 30th, 1862.jpg|left|thumb|[[Burning of Uxiu|Destruction]] of the [[Cabildo of Uxiu]] on the night of 6 June 1837 by Confederal forces]]
Despite controlling a numerical troop advantage throughout the war, the Bayarans were unable to overcome their geographic disadvantages: the republic was relatively flat and exposed, and secessionist forces were unable to establish control over most high mountain passes across the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], and thus were unable to directly threaten Hesperian territory. Bayaran armies crossed the [[Angostura]] and attempted to capture the [[Alpujarra]], culminating in the [[Siege of Castejón]], which was defeated by the deployment of [[Araucaria|Araucarian]] territorial forces — the former Maroon militias, now fighting as a constituent republic of the confederation under the terms of the [[Convention of Jatiel]] — alongside northern confederal regiments. Bayara suffered substantially more than the rest of the Confederation overall, as the confederal strategy of {{W|Attrition warfare|attrition warfare}} meant that Bayaran forces could not replace their casualties as confederal armies devastated the infrastructure, causing widespread poverty and distress.

The Carpinchera Republic and the rest of Bayara were reintegrated into the Northern Confederation after the secessionists' defeat at the [[Battle of Punta Lagunas]] in 1837. The subsequent [[Treaty of Bixessarri]] (1838) punished Bayara severely: the republic was forced to pay the expenses and debts incurred by the Confederation during the war, the General Junta was coerced into ratifying the Compromise of 1827, and significant amounts of land in Bayara's north were ceded — including [[Trás-as-Montanyas]] (ceded to Hesperia) and the northern wetlands (allocated to the now-independent Estuarian community). The treaty also revoked the Maroon removal policy, facilitated the [[W:Right of return|right of return]] for displaced populations, and mandated reparations in the form of [[W:Land redistribution|land redistribution]] — the very redistribution that Bayara's planter class had seceded to prevent. The recognition of [[W:Indigenous sovereignty|Indigenous sovereignty]] and constitutional guarantees of cultural and political rights for all communities, backed by aggressive confederal protection, completed the dismantling of the racial hierarchy that had defined Bayaran society.

=== Reconstruction and the Federal Years ===
{{Main|Anyos Federales}}

The Reconstruction period saw the transformation of Bayaran alienation from a regional grievance into a structural condition of confederal governance. Caudillo [[Michelangelo Valentino]], who had served as the country's first president since 1838, oversaw a dramatic expansion of federal authority, using the powers granted to him during the [[Confederal emergency|civil war emergency]] to consolidate control under the guise of directing reconstruction. This centralization program represented the solidification of the break with the decentralized traditions that had characterized Baliscan governance before the civil war, when substantial autonomy had been reserved to the Republics.

Valentino's administration pursued a program of systematic centralization across multiple domains that fundamentally altered the relationship between the federal government and the republics. Economically, the federal government assumed control over customs revenue, which had previously been shared with the provinces, and directed investment toward the capital region of [[Castejón]] and the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]], at the expense of the interior. This concentration of resources in the capital created deep resentment in western territories, which saw their wealth and resources being extracted to Castejón while receiving little in return. Within the republic of Bayara there was still widespread resentment from the destruction wrought upon it during the Baliscan Civil War, and the community had historically maintained strong regional identities and traditions of self-governance, which proved particularly resistant to this economic centralization.

Politically, Valentino and his cabinet built an extensive network of allies through the [[W:Spoils system|distribution of government positions and contracts]], creating a class of dependent elites whose fortunes were tied to the continuation of his administration. The president's patronage extended from high-level ministerial appointments to local postmasters, ensuring that a vast web of officials owed their positions to his favor. This system marginalized traditional provincial elites who had previously exercised significant influence in their republics, replacing them with men whose primary loyalty was to Valentino personally rather than to their local communities.

The military dimension of Valentino's centralization proved particularly contentious. The confederal army, which had grown to 250,000 men during the civil war, was retained as a permanent federal force rather than being demobilized or returned to provincial control as many had expected. This provided the president with the means to enforce federal authority across the country, but it also represented a constant reminder of federal power in regions that had previously maintained their own military forces. Furthermore, the federal government claimed ownership of all railways and telegraph lines, giving it control over both economic and information flows. This allowed the federal government to move troops rapidly to suppress dissent and to control the spread of news.

For Bayarans, Valentino's centralization compounded the grievances of Reconstruction: not only had the confederal government abolished their labor system, redistributed their land, and imposed racial equality by decree, but it now concentrated the wealth generated by these transformations in the capital and the Levante, while the permanent federal army ensured that resistance was futile. The Federal Years thus completed the structural conditions of Bayaran alienation — a region that had been devastated, restructured, and then economically marginalized by the very government that claimed to be reconstructing it.

== Modern expressions ==

=== Economic divergence and the Silicón Coast ===

{{See also|Silicon Coast|Levante, Balisca}}

The emergence of the tech industry in the Baliscan Levante, the Silicon Coast, in the late 20th and early 21st centuries, especially with the sustained economic degrowth since the [[Special Period]], has dramatically widened the economic gap between the east and west coasts. The emergence of the tech industry as the economic engine of Balisca reinforced the perception among Bayarans that the confederal government's investment priorities had not changed since Valentino's era: wealth and innovation were concentrated in Castejón and the Levante, while Bayara remained a net exporter of raw materials and agricultural products with little confederal investment in the industries of the future.

=== Political movements ===

Bayaran alienation has manifested in a distinctive political tradition that spans the ideological spectrum. On the left, the [[Bastan!]] platform's democratic confederalist principles — emphasizing communal autonomy, decentralized governance, and direct democracy — draw explicitly on the tradition of Bayaran resistance to centralization, while repudiating the racial hierarchy that characterized earlier expressions of Bayaran autonomy. On the right, the [[Reconquista (political party)|''Reconquista!'']] exile movement and its descendants have drawn on Bayaran alienation to construct narratives of confederal tyranny that explicitly romanticize the pre-Reconstruction social order.

The [[Shadow authoritarianism|shadow authoritarianism]] conspiracy theory, which alleges that the [[Confederal Left]] operates as a clandestine authoritarian government, has found particularly fertile ground in Bayara, where the confederal government's institutions — the [[Digital sol|digital sol]], the [[Social Protection Service]], and the [[Secretariat for the Future]] — are experienced not as democratic innovations but as the latest iteration of a centuries-long pattern of external control over Bayaran life. The irony that shadow authoritarianism is a right-wing conspiracy theory while Bayara's own tradition of resistance is fundamentally anti-statist has not prevented its adoption, particularly among the ''[[Desconexión]]'' subculture, which shares the conspiracy theory's distrust of institutional power while rejecting its political conclusions.

== Legacy ==
Bayaran alienation remains one of the most politically sensitive topics in Baliscan historiography. The central difficulty is one of moral complexity: the grievances are real — Bayara was devastated by wars it did not start, economically marginalized by policies it did not choose, and subjected to a reconstruction that, however just in its aims, was imposed by military force. At the same time, the most extreme expressions of Bayaran alienation — the secession of 1828, the Maroon removal campaign, the black codes — were themselves acts of [[W:Racial violence|racial violence]] and ethnic cleansing that the confederal government had a moral obligation to prevent. 

The question of whether Bayaran alienation is a legitimate response to structural marginalization or a reframing of grievance that obscures the defense of racial hierarchy — or, more commonly, some combination of both — continues to animate scholarly and political debate.

